    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(7))  # F0..F6\n    customers = list(range(7))  # C0..C6\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(87, 18), (65, 86), (20, 53), (91, 61), (18, 86), (14, 45), (79, 37)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(52, 78), (49, 47), (87, 18), (54, 32), (67, 33), (64, 80), (95, 6)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [153.0, 59.0, 58.0, 133.0, 140.0, 183.0, 199.0]\n    customer_demands = [38.0, 38.0, 17.0, 17.0, 16.0, 21.0, 10.0]\n    fixed_costs = [492.0, 220.0, 209.0, 145.0, 253.0, 349.0, 348.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Framing the warehouse location and distribution decision\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at the point where we need to decide which warehouses to open and how to serve our markets at the lowest overall cost. We have seven candidate sites and seven customer locations. The decision is twofold: (1) which facilities to open, and (2) how much each open facility should ship to each customer.\n\nHere\u2019s how the economics work in plain terms. Opening a facility comes with a one-time fixed cost. Once a facility is open, the cost to move product depends on how far it travels and how many units we ship. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates, so shorter geographic paths directly reduce shipping spend. Shipping cost increases in proportion to both the distance and the volume moved, based on the transport rate. The overall objective is to minimize the total of the opening costs plus the shipping costs while meeting all service needs.\n\nBusiness rules and practical constraints:\n- A facility can only ship if it\u2019s opened.\n- Each customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n- No facility can ship more than its capacity.\n- We will evaluate trade-offs between opening additional sites (to shorten average delivery distances) versus consolidating shipments through fewer locations (to avoid extra fixed costs).\n\nInformation provided for this decision:\n- Candidate facility locations (map coordinates): Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (87, 18)\n  - F1: (65, 86)\n  - F2: (20, 53)\n  - F3: (91, 61)\n  - F4: (18, 86)\n  - F5: (14, 45)\n  - F6: (79, 37)\n- Customer locations (map coordinates): Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (52, 78)\n  - C1: (49, 47)\n  - C2: (87, 18)\n  - C3: (54, 32)\n  - C4: (67, 33)\n  - C5: (64, 80)\n  - C6: (95, 6)\n- Facility capacity limits: [153.0, 59.0, 58.0, 133.0, 140.0, 183.0, 199.0]\n- Customer demand requirements: [38.0, 38.0, 17.0, 17.0, 16.0, 21.0, 10.0]\n- Facility opening (fixed) costs: [492.0, 220.0, 209.0, 145.0, 253.0, 349.0, 348.0]\n- Transport rate per kilometer per unit shipped: 0.5\n\nWhat the outcome will look like:\n- A recommended set of facilities to open that balances fixed spend and delivery efficiency.\n- A shipment plan specifying how much each open facility should send to each customer to meet all demand within capacity.\n- A clear projection of total cost under the recommended plan, with visibility into how the choice of locations affects shipping distances and spend.\n\nOnce we align on the assumptions above, we\u2019ll run the analysis and bring back a recommendation that shows the cost, service, and risk implications of the top alternatives.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Framing the warehouse location and distribution decision\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at the point where we need to decide which warehouses to open and how to serve our markets at the lowest overall cost. We have seven candidate sites and seven customer locations. The decision is twofold: (1) which facilities to open, and (2) how much each open facility should ship to each customer.\n\nHere\u2019s how the economics work in plain terms. Opening a facility comes with a one-time fixed cost. Once a facility is open, the cost to move product depends on how far it travels and how many units we ship. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates, so shorter geographic paths directly reduce shipping spend. Shipping cost increases in proportion to both the distance and the volume moved, based on the transport rate. The overall objective is to minimize the total of the opening costs plus the shipping costs while meeting all service needs.\n\nBusiness rules and practical constraints:\n- A facility can only ship if it\u2019s opened.\n- Each customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n- No facility can ship more than its capacity.\n- We will evaluate trade-offs between opening additional sites (to shorten average delivery distances) versus consolidating shipments through fewer locations (to avoid extra fixed costs).\n\nInformation provided for this decision:\n- Candidate facility locations (map coordinates): {FACILITY_COORDINATES}\n- Customer locations (map coordinates): {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}\n- Facility capacity limits: {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}\n- Customer demand requirements: {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}\n- Facility opening (fixed) costs: {FIXED_COSTS}\n- Transport rate per kilometer per unit shipped: {TRANSPORT_RATE}\n\nWhat the outcome will look like:\n- A recommended set of facilities to open that balances fixed spend and delivery efficiency.\n- A shipment plan specifying how much each open facility should send to each customer to meet all demand within capacity.\n- A clear projection of total cost under the recommended plan, with visibility into how the choice of locations affects shipping distances and spend.\n\nOnce we align on the assumptions above, we\u2019ll run the analysis and bring back a recommendation that shows the cost, service, and risk implications of the top alternatives."
